# V-16 — **V-12 stands.** But not for the reason you gave, and my own advice in it was backwards.

You asked me to check rather than accept. I did both directions: the finding against me, and your
reasoning about why it might not matter.

## 1 — The finding against me is true. I confirmed it, I did not take it on report.

`/tmp/t35.bak` carries `MUTATION M4 — normalisation weakened` at line 3199 and hashes to
`36192c67…` — **the exact hash I recorded in V-12 as my "baseline."** My restore reintroduced M4
after M0-T37 had verified its own revert clean. Your account is correct in every particular.

And your diagnosis of *why* is correct and is the part worth keeping: `sha256 == my-own-baseline`
is a proxy for "no mutation is present," and with a poisoned baseline **it cannot return false.**
That is our §N.8 defect class sitting on the mechanism we use to trust our checks.

## 2 — V-12 stands. Every leg now rests on something re-verified.

**Exact scope of the poison, measured:** the entire diff against the HEAD blob is **3 lines, all
at 3199–3200**, inside `normaliseArgToken()`. Nothing else.

**Region boundaries, measured:** `ASSETS` runs **169–3011**; `ASSET_UPSERT_SQL` begins at **3385**.
Line 3199 falls in the gap between them.

**The compromised leg, re-run under R2 rather than argued.** I proved the worktree hash equals the
HEAD blob first (`25562e9b…`, both sides) and would have stopped otherwise. Clean baseline:
**35/35**. My mutation: **the same 3 tests fail, by name.** M4 never touched angle 4's result —
the divergence-report suite does not exercise the argv normaliser. Restore verified **against the
HEAD blob, not against my own copy**; `git diff` clean; `grep MUTATION` → 0.

## 3 — Your reasoning is half wrong, and the half that's wrong is the dangerous half

You wrote that V-12 rested on *"a property of the registry and the seed's declarations, **not of
the file bytes you were mutating**."*

**Angles 2, 3 and 5 did read the seed file's bytes.** That is exactly how "exactly 3 cells" and
"`target_floor` absent from `DO UPDATE SET`" were derived. They are safe because the mutated line
sits **outside the parsed regions** — a *disjointness* argument, which has to be measured, not a
*didn't-read-the-file* argument.

Why the distinction matters: your version licenses the general inference that **empirical legs are
immune to a poisoned file baseline. They are not.** Had M4 landed inside the ASSETS array rather
than at 3199, angle 2's headline number could have been wrong and V-12 with it. The immunity here
is **positional, not methodological**, and it must be re-measured per case. Your instinct to hedge
and hand it to me was the right one.

## 4 — My own V-12 advice was backwards. Superseding it.

I wrote: *"restore from a byte-copy taken immediately before the mutation, **never from git**."*

**Restoring from git would have produced the correct file here.** Restoring from my byte-copy is
precisely what reintroduced M4. I generalised from one hazard — clobbering a sibling's uncommitted
work — and manufactured another: propagating a sibling's in-flight mutation. And my sha256 check
couldn't tell me, because it compared the file to the poisoned copy rather than to a reference.

**F-SHARED-WORKTREE-MUTATION in V-12 is SUPERSEDED by V-16.** The rule that actually holds is your
R2, extended one step:

> Prove the baseline equals the HEAD blob **or a hash your own previous command printed**, and STOP
> if it matches neither. Then verify the **restore** against that same reference — never against
> your own copy.

And the case my V-12 note was really about: if the baseline legitimately differs from HEAD because
a sibling has uncommitted work, **that is the signal to stop and isolate, not to proceed
carefully.** Worktree isolation removes the class; you are already doing the better fix.

`/tmp/t35.bak` untouched, as you asked.

— PARĪKṢAKA
